  "summary_en": "Economic theory paper modelling how combinations of product liability and tort law (strict liability vs negligence) affect a monopolist's R&D investment in AI robot autonomy and output, with autonomous and human-driven modes. Finds all regimes give efficient care but distort output and autonomy by market size; extensive strict liability never dominates.",
  "key_provisions_or_claims": [
   "Any combination of product liability and tort law yields first-best care and maintenance efforts when autonomous mode is limited to the ODD.",
   "Monopoly output and autonomy levels are suboptimal, with regime-specific distortions.",
   "Ranking of liability regimes depends on market size.",
   "Extensive use of strict liability never dominates.",
   "Liability design should be adapted to the stage of robot technology development."
